pasztar / arcana

This is the 4th iteration of a messaging app, that started 6 years ago as a school project.
I wanted to finally make something out of it so I went with a node/local access model.
You host it on your VPS and connect over the VPN like WireGuard or Tailscale.

VPN-only deployment is strongly preferred
If latency issues occur, trusted identities can be enabled and served over the internet.

The app is served by Docker Compose. The browser still
needs HTTPS because WebCrypto is unavailable on plain HTTP origins.

1. Configure env:

  ./scripts/setup-bootstrap-token.sh pasztar.example.com

Use the WireGuard server IP for the app bindings:

  FRONTEND_PORT=10.8.0.1:8080
  API_PORT=10.8.0.1:8000

Use the same WireGuard IP for TURN:

  TURN_EXTERNAL_IP=10.8.0.1
  TURN_ALLOWED_PEER_RANGE=10.8.0.0-10.8.0.255
  TURN_PORT=10.8.0.1:3478
  TURN_RELAY_PORTS=10.8.0.1:49160-49200
  CALL_ICE_SERVERS=[{"urls":["stun:10.8.0.1:3478","turn:10.8.0.1:3478?transport=udp","turn:10.8.0.1:3478?transport=tcp"]}]

2. Start the app:

  docker compose up -d --build

3. Point DNS at the public server IP so Caddy can get a trusted cert:

  pasztar.example.com A <server-public-ip>

4. Add the Caddy reverse proxy:

  pasztar.example.com {
    @not_vpn {
      not remote_ip 10.8.0.0/24 127.0.0.1 ::1
    }
    respond @not_vpn 403

    reverse_proxy 10.8.0.1:8080
  }

5. Validate and reload Caddy:

  sudo caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
  sudo systemctl reload caddy

6. Open the app while connected to WireGuard:

  https://pasztar.example.com

Joining and inviting users

With TRUSTED_IDENTITIES=true, open the setup URL printed by the bootstrap script,
or paste its token into the setup page, and create the first identity. The token
is consumed once. In Settings, choose "Invite someone" and copy the invitation
link to share it yourself. Each invitation admits one identity and expires after
INVITATION_LIFETIME_HOURS (24 by default). Anyone holding the link can redeem it, and every admitted user can
invite others. Recipients still need VPN access to reach the app.

Already-registered identities retain access and can invite users when trusted
mode is enabled. Importing an existing identity bundle needs no new token.
TRUSTED_IDENTITIES=false keeps registration open and hides invitation controls.

The bootstrap script's --rotate option replaces an unused bootstrap token;
restart/recreate the backend to load the new environment. Rotation or a server
restart does not reopen bootstrap after an identity has joined. Export your
identity bundle from Settings for recovery. An empty trusted server without a
BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN rejects registration until the operator configures one.

If the page returns 403 while connected to WireGuard, browser traffic is reaching
Caddy from a public IP instead of 10.8.0.0/24. Use full-tunnel WireGuard, route
<server-public-ip>/32 through WireGuard, or use split DNS so pasztar.example.com
resolves to 10.8.0.1 for VPN clients.

Internet deployment

Run the bootstrap script before exposing the app. It enables TRUSTED_IDENTITIES
and generates TURN_SHARED_SECRET if absent or still set to the example value.
Existing identities retain access; review identities admitted during open registration.

Keep API_PORT=127.0.0.1:8000 and FRONTEND_PORT=127.0.0.1:8080. Publish HTTPS
through Caddy using this public-site configuration:

  pasztar.example.com {
    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
  }

Set TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDR to the exact address/CIDR of your Caddy proxy as seen by
the frontend container (often the Docker network gateway for a host-side proxy).
Only this peer may supply X-Forwarded-For for per-client limits. The default is
127.0.0.1/32; without the correct value, proxied users share one rate bucket.
Do not trust arbitrary internet addresses, and keep the frontend port private.

Nginx settings come from environment variables via its native config template:
API_REQUEST_RATE/BURST (10r/s, 30), REGISTRATION_REQUEST_RATE/BURST (5r/m, 5),
and API_CONNECTIONS_PER_IP (20). Admission limits apply only to POST /api/clients.
API_MAX_BODY_BYTES defaults to 524288; only /api/messages accepts the larger
MESSAGE_MAX_BODY_BYTES (178257920). CLIENT_BODY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS defaults to 30.
EVENT_PROXY_TIMEOUT_SECONDS (75) must exceed EVENT_KEEPALIVE_SECONDS (25).
Nginx streams events without response buffering. Keep the API port private so
requests cannot bypass these limits. A shared NAT shares one IP quota.

For public calls, bind TURN_PORT and TURN_RELAY_PORTS to the public interface,
set TURN_EXTERNAL_IP to its public IP, and use that host in CALL_ICE_SERVERS.
Permit the configured TURN TCP/UDP listener and UDP relay range in the firewall.
TURN_SHARED_SECRET must be a strong secret shared only by the API and coturn;
CALL_ICE_SERVERS should contain URLs without static usernames or passwords.
The API supplies identity-bound credentials lasting TURN_CREDENTIALS_LIFETIME_SECONDS
(3600), refreshed on call start/reconnect. Existing TURN_PASSWORD is accepted
as a migration fallback for the shared secret; replace it with TURN_SHARED_SECRET.

TURN_USER_QUOTA/TURN_TOTAL_QUOTA default to 8/100 allocations. TURN_MAX_BPS and
TURN_BPS_CAPACITY default to 2000000 and 20000000 bytes/second respectively.
Private, link-local and loopback peers are denied; TURN_ALLOWED_PEER_RANGE can
permit your specific VPN subnet when needed. It defaults to TURN_EXTERNAL_IP.
Recreate containers after changing environment values.

Identity inactivity and cleanup

After IDENTITY_INACTIVE_DAYS (7), identities appear under the collapsed
"Inactive identities" section. Their chats remain accessible. A successfully
authenticated request updates activity and restores the identity to the main list.
Receiving messages alone does not keep an identity active.

After IDENTITY_CLEANUP_DAYS (90) without authenticated activity, the server
permanently deletes that identity and all messages to/from it, including the
other participant's copy, plus its calls, signals, invitations and nonces.
Older messages between retained identities are preserved. Browser exports and
external backups are outside this cleanup. Returning after deletion requires
a new invitation; importing the old bundle does not restore deleted history.

Cleanup runs at startup and every IDENTITY_CLEANUP_INTERVAL_SECONDS (3600),
processing at most IDENTITY_CLEANUP_BATCH_SIZE (100) identities per pass.
Deletion is transactional and serialized against authenticated activity.
Pydantic requires positive settings and cleanup days greater than inactive days.
Expired invitations and nonces are also pruned. Cleanup never reopens bootstrap,
even when the last identity expires; an empty previously used node requires
operator recovery rather than accepting the old bootstrap token again.
